# SwiftPM build cache (sdks/swift) — same role as node_modules/target/.gradle
# above: regenerated by `swift build`/`swift test`, never committed.
.build/

# Compiled Go example binary (`go build` output in examples/go/)
examples/go/go

# Bun writes this when `bun install` runs in sdks/node. The lockfile of record
# for sdks/node is npm's package-lock.json — `bun install --frozen-lockfile`
# reads it directly (verified), so a second lockfile would only add drift.
# sdks/web is the opposite: bun IS its toolchain, so sdks/web/bun.lock is
# tracked (not matched by this node-scoped pattern) and sdks/web/package-lock.json
# is the one ignored below instead.
sdks/node/bun.lock
sdks/node/bun.lockb

# npm writes this when `npm install`/`npm pack` touch sdks/web (package build
# dry runs, editor tooling). The lockfile of record for sdks/web is bun's
# bun.lock — bun is the only toolchain sdks/web's scripts use (Task 11 phase-5
# restructure: web flatten dropped the tracked package-lock.json in favor of
# this ignore, matching node's npm/bun split the other way around).
sdks/web/package-lock.json

# Node conformance runner's default report path (sdks/node/test/conformance/run.ts,
# no --out given). Untracked deliberately (task-10 review round 2, finding 1): a
# committed default would let a stale report from a prior run silently masquerade
# as fresh if the runner ever crashed before reaching its write. Unlike node,
# go/python/java's own committed compatibility-report.<lang>.json snapshots are
# refreshed only by an explicit, human-run standalone command (CONTRIBUTING.md)
# and are never read back by scripts/conformance-all.sh (it always passes each of
# them an explicit ephemeral -out/--out path) — that asymmetry is intentional and
# scoped to this fix; see task-10-report.md.
sdks/node/test/conformance/compatibility-report.node.json

# Same rationale as node's entry above (task-10 review round 2, finding 1),
# extended to the other six languages (final fix wave, C-report snapshot
# cleanup): these compatibility-report.<lang>.json files are never read by
# scripts/conformance-all.sh (it always passes each language's runner an
# explicit ephemeral -out/--out path) and are refreshed only by an explicit,
# human-run standalone command (CONTRIBUTING.md). A committed snapshot reads
# as authoritative but drifts the moment the real fixtures move on.
sdks/go/conformance/compatibility-report.go.json
sdks/java/fireweave-testing/compatibility-report.java.json
sdks/python/conformance/compatibility-report.python.json
sdks/rust/conformance/compatibility-report.rust.json
sdks/swift/conformance/compatibility-report.swift.json
sdks/web/test/conformance/compatibility-report.web.json

# .superpowers/ holds task briefs + review diffs for this implementation
# effort (~6 MB) — not project source, never meant to ship. A nested
# .gitignore at .superpowers/sdd/.gitignore already ignores everything
# under sdd/, but that's brittle (misses anything added directly under
# .superpowers/, or if that nested file is ever removed); this root entry
# is the authoritative one.
.superpowers/
